One concern: failingWriter embeds http.ResponseWriter; gin checks for http.Hijacker/Flusher/etc. via interface assertions on the wrapped writer? gin wraps our writer in its own responseWriter which implements everything by delegating to `w.ResponseWriter` with type assertions at call time — only called if used. Not used here. Also `WriteHeader` passes through to recorder. Recorder records 201. We don't assert on recorder.Code since the point is the client got nothing usable; body len 0 is the assertion. Actually, should I assert recorder.Code? In real life the status line may or may not have been sent. httptest records WriteHeader(201). Asserting Code==201 would be "the server attempted 201" — that's fine and actually meaningful: server-side it generated a 201. But careful: gin's responseWriter.WriteHeader(201) is called during render; our failingWriter only fails Write, not WriteHeader. So recorder.Code == 201. I could assert it to show "server generated 201 but delivery failed". Hmm — but the doc says don't promise the client received a status. The recorder is the transport stand-in; asserting Code==201 means "server wrote the status line into the transport". I'll assert body empty only, and mention in comments that whether the status line left the server is transport-dependent. Actually asserting recorder.Code == 201 is deterministic here and documents "server-side 201 was generated". I'll include it with a comment that this is the server-side result, not a client guarantee.

